#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "e4f2ef63-cc21-5aeb-b1c4-6a7a4d40e81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12411" :question/problem "Use spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$ for $y \\geq 0$ and $z \\leq 0$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/5654932cdfd91d86596d690f06295ce4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/56b93c553c9a936450ae7f7f62aa0966.png)\n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}, \\: {0} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\pi}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}2} \\leq \\phi \\leq {\\pi} \\right\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "68b95ae7-c0ac-590c-a764-0500455ca70c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12412" :question/problem "Use spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/a486e3c62a157d0581ad2221d2947aab.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0\n$$\n\nin spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 4z &= 0 \\\\\nρ^{2} + 4ρ\\cos ϕ &= 0 \\\\\nρ(ρ + 4\\cos ϕ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have\n\n$$\n\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/136ebbc722e56fc5a4c38e5789005004.png)\n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: -\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175738 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "78981c7f-ec42-5eac-afb8-145bd2a7e66c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8811" :question/problem "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/96e952bf87ea43bab876f5dbde7660cd.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nradians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175739 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "bc7cbddf-7c15-5762-b035-cbd68a1f25f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8812" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho=5$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5$. On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/5be9e8b03cf13d47fe1fbf136b253c63.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988c0c53-e5a4-5f3e-add0-18ee4a4f054b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173311} {:answer/id #uuid "a71bed2c-0ee9-5924-9fee-80c92e9f69c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173312} {:answer/id #uuid "c03ba347-80cc-570d-bdfd-17a999b994fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173313} {:answer/id #uuid "0663dd61-8117-58e8-8294-916c80615a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173314} {:answer/id #uuid "3c421c89-baa7-58b0-9858-c0ae6e816547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71bed2c-0ee9-5924-9fee-80c92e9f69c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173312} :answer-field/id #uuid "21447bc9-f05d-5935-be7c-f70c0e1747cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173310}] :question/id #uuid "4f0566ac-a9c9-553f-9d54-910954e3b64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102949" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/q-102949.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?"}]}
